{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q008_v1_atp_synthase_bulge_classification","answer":"Verified SCOPe 2.08 facts: the bulge domain is not split off from the other barrel-sandwich-hybrid members by a separate fold. It is one of five superfamilies under fold b.84 (Barrel-sandwich hybrid, under class b, All beta proteins): b.84.1 Single hybrid motif, b.84.2 Rudiment single hybrid motif, b.84.3 Duplicated hybrid motif, b.84.4 Ribosomal L27 protein-like, and b.84.5 'V1 ATP synthase A subunit, bulge domain-like' (sunid 310577). The bulge lineage is b > b.84 > b.84.5 > family b.84.5.1 'V1 ATP synthase A subunit, bulge domain-like' (sunid 310616) > protein 'V1 ATP synthase A subunit, bulge domain' (sunid 310706, sccs b.84.5.1). A sibling protein, 'A1 ATP synthase A subunit, bulge domain' (sunid 310707), sits in the same family. Domain entries retrieved by search, including d1vdza2, d3a5ca3 and d3j9ta3, are also b.84.5.1.

What the placement claims: SCOPe's superfamily-level homology claim applies to b.84.5 (homology_implied_by_level is true, homology_scope sf, no warnings or heterogeneity flags). So the claim is that bulge-domain members of the V1/A1 A-subunit family share common ancestry at the superfamily level. Placing them in a separate superfamily also means SCOPe does not assert superfamily-level common ancestry between the bulge domain and b.84.1 to b.84.4. That is a lack of a positive homology assertion, not evidence that they are unrelated or convergent. The shared fold is a structural-architecture statement (the fold annotation reads 'sandwich of half-barrel shaped beta-sheets'). SCOPe's own warning says levels above superfamily do not imply homology, so the fold-level relationship to the other hybrid-motif superfamilies is structural similarity only.

Why it is separate: the API does not state the curatorial rationale. The only annotation on b.84.5 is a PubMed reference (19893485). I could not retrieve or read that paper, so the specific sequence or structural reason for the split is not verified here. I also did not compare a bulge representative with any b.84.1 to b.84.4 representative by FATCAT or residue alignment, and I did not query earlier releases, so I cannot say whether or when this split was made. Function is not established by this placement either: I cannot infer the bulge domain's role in V1 rotary catalysis, substrate, or ligand from the superfamily name. To settle the rationale, one would need the cited paper's structural analysis and a release-matched structural comparison between the bulge superfamily and the hybrid-motif superfamilies.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","mcp__scope__search_scop (release 2.08)","mcp__scope__get_scop_node (sunid 310577 with parents, annotations, homology)","mcp__scope__get_scop_node (sunid 51229 with annotations, homology)","mcp__scope__get_scop_node (sunid 310706 with parents, annotations, homology)","mcp__scope__list_scop_children (sunid 51229, limit 20, has_more false)","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=bulge","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310577","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310577/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310577/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310577/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51229","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51229/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51229/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51229/children?limit=20","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310706","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310706/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310706/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/310706/homology"]}]}
